commit 5013b24a837104a4ad570bb080d211c185ebd702
Author: Martin Oberzalek <kingleo@gmx.at>
Date:   Thu Sep 24 16:18:12 2026 +0200

    Make the golden CLI oracle portable across machines
    
    tests/golden/oracle-cli.json was committed with the absolute paths of the machine
    it was captured on, so --check could only ever pass on that one checkout, and the
    repository carried a developer's drive letter and temp directory in every argv.
    
    * tools/rfa_golden.py - tokenise in a defined order: scratch root, then the named
      substitutions, then the repo root, so a fixture path without a token of its own
      still reduces to a {REPO_ROOT}-prefixed one. Also token out the
      `rfa_file_name: A <- B` heap addresses as {ADDR}: that line is process layout,
      not behaviour - it shifts with the size of the environment block, so it differs
      between a Cygwin and a PowerShell invocation - and left in it would keep the
      golden unreproducible everywhere but the shell it was captured from. {MS} and
      {REPO_ROOT} documented as tokens.
    * tools/rfa_oracle.py - refuse to run under a non-Windows interpreter. Cygwin's
      Python hands the native oracle POSIX paths it cannot open, and the failure is
      silent: rfaPack prints "TotalFiles: 0" and still exits 0, so a stray regenerate
      would have committed empty golden files as a fresh baseline.
    * tests/golden/oracle-cli.json - regenerated with the tokens; no absolute paths
      remain.
    * ChangeLog - catch up with the two preceding commits.
    
    make check stays green.

commit 8ce996c3d4d8a7f72f5d6e91256abe643fcc8548
Author: Martin Oberzalek <kingleo@gmx.at>
Date:   Thu Sep 24 16:18:04 2026 +0200

    Stop git from rewriting line endings in the golden fixture tree
    
    Two files of the writer fixture tree hold CRLF on purpose, and the committed
    oracle archives were built from them, so their exact sizes are baked into the
    byte comparison. With core.autocrlf=input - as set on this machine - git stripped
    the CR on commit, which made our writer's output look two bytes short per file and
    turned a passing comparison into a mystery.
    
    tests/data/golden/tree/** is now marked -text, which disables conversion in both
    directions: the working tree keeps whatever bytes the fixture has, and so does the
    repository. The two files are restored to CRLF, which is what they were when the
    oracle archives were generated from them.

commit 7be59609e5a9de62c2b11c89b896dd237c666ce5
Author: Martin Oberzalek <martin.oberzalek@anton-paar.com>
Date:   Wed Sep 23 14:47:49 2026 +0200

    Probe the original rfaPack -u semantics
    
    -u was the last unknown in the plan. Nothing in the vendor readme or in the CLI
    chatter explains it, so it was established black-box with controlled archives and
    trees: tools/rfa_probe_update.py, every result re-extracted by the original to prove
    the archive stays readable.
    
    The headline finding removes most of the work: -u is byte-for-byte a FRESH PACK of
    the source tree using the TARGET ARCHIVE's compression policy, plus entries carried
    over for names absent from the tree. Verified by byte comparison in every case, not
    inferred from reading the output. So -u needs no in-place update algorithm at all; it
    reuses the fresh-pack path already proven byte-identical to the oracle.
    
    Also established:
    
    * -Compress is IGNORED on -u, in both directions. -u -Compress leaves a store archive
      raw (version stays 0), and plain -u adds COMPRESSED entries to a compress archive
      (version stays 1). The flag is parsed and echoed as UseCompression: N and never
      reaches the archive.
    * -u never deletes. An entry missing from the tree is retained and still extracted.
    * -u with no existing archive just creates it. rc is 0 in every case.
    
    Two destructive behaviours, both found only because the probe compared against a
    fresh pack and varied the base name:
    
    * -u RENAMES carried-over entries as newBase + "/" + storedName.substr(len(newBase)+1).
      On the FH archive, which stores bf1942/levels/..., base bf1942 leaves the names
      untouched, base menu yields menu/2/levels/... (the stray 2 is the tail of the eaten
      bf194), and base bf1942x yields bf1942x/evels/... . The rule was stated as a
      prediction and then confirmed at three base lengths. We deliberately do NOT
      reproduce this (D7): it renames files unasked, and it is invisible in the documented
      workflow because that workflow always passes the matching base name.
    * -u REPLACES a foreign reserved region. The FH archive's is 4078 bytes at offset
      8..4086; a single -u normalised it to the standard 148 bytes at offset 156,
      discarding about 3.9 KB. This retires the "an in-place -u must preserve the
      target's bytes here" claim in rfa/RfaStamp.h, which was wrong - and could not have
      been detected with self-produced archives, where the region already is the constant
      (D8).
    * Opaque per-entry fields are NOT preserved: setting flags to 0xFFFFFFFF and reserved2
      to 0xDEADBEEF and then running -u reset every entry to 0. On the FH archive one -u
      collapsed all its 0xFFFFFFFF and 0x7C001CD8 values to 0. That contradicts the advice
      to "treat flags as opaque and preserve it on -u" which was in AGENTS.md, the skills
      and the code comments; all are corrected (D6).
    
    Documentation corrected beyond the -u material: the entry-order rule in the plan's own
    findings section and in the format skill still claimed "ascending by name (ASCII)",
    which Phase 2 had already disproved. Also fixes the same less-than-versus-inequality
    chunk rule in tools/rfa_probe.py, which had escaped the earlier sweep.
    
    Entry table layout recorded while tracing this: u32 entryCount, then per entry
    u32 nameLen + name + 24 bytes, then a trailing zero u32; reserved1 is 1253856
    (0x001321E0).
    
    rfa/RfaStamp.h is generated, so the corrected note lives in its generator's template
    as well, and the file was regenerated to prove it round-trips.
    
    make check -> PASS, 59/59.

commit 8928787c275f5aac1397381dd414065194d51e06
