    fix the stale less-than chunk test in the CLI reference
    
    The rfa-format doc had the same incorrect rule corrected in the Phase 2 commit; an
    entry's chunk is an LZO1X stream iff compressedSize != uncompressedSize, because
    LZO1X expands small or incompressible chunks.

commit f235cc4efd096c2f4cc2ea45d29be1bf50700fb7
Author: Martin Oberzalek <martin.oberzalek@anton-paar.com>
Date:   Wed Sep 23 13:58:40 2026 +0200

    Phase 2 (part 2): RfaWriter, oracle byte-comparison and golden archives
    
    Completes the packer and the compatibility evidence for Phase 2 in
    PLAN_rfa_tools.md.  make check -> PASS, 59/59 testcases, no diagnostics.
    
    * rfa/RfaWriter.h/.cc - packs a directory tree in either policy, reproducing
      rfaPack.exe's layout: version 0 + raw entries without -Compress, version 1 +
      32 KiB chunked entries with it, data at offset 156 behind the stamp, entries
      in the oracle's walk order.  A failed pack removes its partial output rather
      than leaving a half-written archive that looks valid.
    * rfa/RfaStamp.h - the 148-byte region rfaPack.exe writes before the first data
      block, generated by tools/rfa_stamp_gen.py.  Its meaning is unknown and no
      reader needs it, but reproducing it makes our archives structurally
      indistinguishable from the tool's.
    * Parallelism: chunk compression runs across a pool sized by
      hardware_concurrency(), one work item per 32 KiB chunk.  Results are indexed
      by chunk rather than appended in completion order, so output cannot depend on
      scheduling; verified byte-identical at 1 and 8 threads.
    * tools/rfa_golden_archives.py + tests/data/golden/ - a small source tree and the
      archives the ORIGINAL rfaPack.exe wrote from it, committed as the writer's
      oracle.  Launching the oracle from a test was not viable: std::system goes
      through cmd.exe, which reads forward slashes as switches and resolves relative
      paths against its own working directory.  Committing them also makes the suite
      hermetic and fast.
    
    Two corrections the tests forced, both of a kind that silently corrupt data:
    
    * Chunk::is_compressed() must test compressedSize != uncompressedSize, NOT
      less-than.  LZO1X expands incompressible input (1 byte becomes 5), so "<"
      classified exactly those chunks as verbatim and returned compressed bytes as
      file content.  Size-only self-consistency cannot see this; only the oracle
      comparison catches it.
    * rfaPack.exe does not sort entries by full path.  Each directory contributes its
      own files (sorted) and then its subdirectories (sorted), recursively.  A
      full-path sort agrees on flat trees and diverges on nested ones; the
      sort_entries option was removed rather than left as a trap.
    
    Store-mode output is byte-identical to rfaPack.exe, which pins the stamp, first
    data offset, reserved field, version, table layout and entry order in a single
    comparison.  Compress-mode output is deliberately compared semantically instead:
    RFA Pack 1.7 embeds a 2003-era LZO that picks different but equally valid matches
    than miniLZO 2.10, and whose empty-file stream is 4 bytes where miniLZO's is 3.
    
    -u in-place update remains unimplemented; its semantics are still unknown and
    must be probed before being written.

commit ada2de5b62bb178a195a3b06f0eef142891a01e2
Author: Martin Oberzalek <martin.oberzalek@anton-paar.com>
Date:   Wed Sep 23 13:40:54 2026 +0200

    Phase 2 (part 1): rfa container model, LZO wrapper and archive reader
    
    Implements steps 1-3 of Phase 2 in PLAN_rfa_tools.md. 48/48 testcases pass.
    
    * rfa/RfaFormat.h - the container model from the 814-archive sweep: CHUNK_SIZE,
      BLOCK_HEADER_SIZE, CHUNK_DESCRIPTOR_SIZE, PayloadVariant, Chunk, Entry and
      chunk_count_for(). Header-only and I/O-free, so the arithmetic is testable in
      isolation from any file handling.
    * rfa/LzoCodec.h/.cc - miniLZO wrapper. An instance owns the compressor work
      buffer, so one per thread; decompress() is static and stateless, which means
      concurrent decompression needs no locking. compress() lets callers fall back to
      storing a chunk verbatim, because a chunk's compressedSize may legitimately
      exceed its uncompressedSize.
    * rfa/RfaArchive.h/.cc - reader for BOTH payload variants. Validates every
      invariant from the format model and collects complaints in problems() rather
      than failing hard. Parsing reads the table and the chunk descriptor tables but
      never a payload, so a 741 MB archive opens cheaply. PayloadReader holds its own
      file handle, which is what makes per-entry parallel extraction safe by
      construction rather than by convention.
    * src_test_rfa/test_rfa_format.{h,cc} - the model arithmetic, including the real
      fixture numbers rather than only round figures.
    * src_test_rfa/test_rfa_archive.{h,cc} - the reader against the fixtures.
    * src_test_rfa/test_lzo.{h,cc} - added LzoCodec wrapper cases.
    
    Coverage: all 259 entries across the four fixtures decompress to exactly their
    declared size, including 92 multi-chunk entries up to 22 chunks / 699,192 bytes,
    and the raw headerless variant. Corrupt input is rejected without crashing:
    truncated, garbage, empty, and payload-corrupted archives.
    
    Two gaps are recorded rather than papered over:
    * no fixture contains an Empty entry, so that variant is still untested; the
      writer will synthesise one.
    * the reader is self-consistent but NOT yet verified against the oracle - Phase 3
      compares against bin/rfaUnpack.orig.exe. The exception is
      archive_conquest_con_matches_phase0_ground_truth, which pins a real 373-byte
      file against bytes the original tool wrote to disk in Phase 0.
    
    RfaWriter, -u update and the multithreaded worker pool remain for part 2.

